pouch boundary-line patch 0050 - a fault signal sent to oneself ends the caller; kill() answers ESRCH and EPERM (signal chunk S-3).

Two kernel changes reach Pouch here (operator votes 2026-10-14). SYS_POSTNOTE
answers each refusal with its own errno instead of a bare -1, which the seam
had read as EIO for every one of them: a missing pid is ESRCH, a process that
is not the caller's child is EACCES, a full note queue is EAGAIN, a name no
program may post is EINVAL. And its self arm accepts the snare a fault signal
names, ending the caller through the kernel's fault path and recording that
signal (docs/ERRORS.md "The snare:* fault-note family"). Four files:

  src/internal/_pouch_signal.h, src/signal/_pouch_signal.c
      __pouch_self_snare maps SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGILL and SIGFPE to
      snare:segv, snare:bus, snare:ill and snare:fpe.
  src/signal/raise.c, src/signal/kill.c    raise() of one of the four, and
      kill() of one to the caller itself (pid 0 or getpid()), post that
      snare to self, so the process dies of the signal and its parent reads
      WTERMSIG as that signal. Before this both answered EINVAL and the
      program ran on. To another process the four stay EINVAL, as SIGABRT
      does: a fault is never another process's to cause. SIGABRT keeps
      abort()'s trap (0049).
  src/signal/kill.c    the kernel's EACCES is POSIX kill's EPERM, set
      directly: -EPERM is -1, the flat sentinel the seam turns into EIO.

The witness is pouch-hello-signals: children that raise(SIGSEGV),
kill(getpid(), SIGBUS), raise(SIGILL) and kill(0, SIGFPE) read WIFSIGNALED
with that signal; kill() of a missing pid reads ESRCH; a child's kill() of
its parent reads EPERM.

